---
layout: layouts/base.njk
permalink: /gallery/
key: works
scripts: ["/js/gallery.js"]
---

{# Every one of the 33 works is a real img element in the served HTML - not injected, not
virtualised, not deferred behind a click. That is the point of YEO-137: the Cycle2 markup this
replaces was 33 empty divs carrying CSS background-image, so a crawler found no image, no alt and no
filename anywhere on the page. loading="lazy" is what makes shipping all 33 free - they cost nothing
until scrolled to. Every path comes from src/_data/gallery.js, so none is hand-typed. The
.gallery-page wrapper and the visually-hidden h1 are YEO-140's and are kept as they are: the page
needs exactly one h1, and the id="main" this element used to carry now belongs to the layout's main
landmark. #}

<div class="gallery-page">
    <h1 class="visually-hidden">Works</h1>
    <div
        id="slideshow"
        role="region"
        aria-roledescription="carousel"
        aria-label="Works"
        tabindex="0"
    >
        {# role="list" is redundant in theory, but list-style:none drops the implicit list role in
        Safari/VoiceOver, and this list is how the number of works gets announced. That only holds
        while the children still present as listitem, which is why the APG slide group sits in an
        element of its own (YEO-144). It used to sit on the list item, where role="group" replaced
        the implicit listitem role: the list was left with no valid children, axe failed
        aria-allowed-role and aria-required-children, and the count this role="list" exists to
        restore was never announced. The APG carousel pattern puts that role on a slide container,
        not on a list item. Do not answer a future axe complaint here by deleting role="list" - that
        silences the rule and loses the announcement with it. #}
        <ul class="slides" role="list">
            {%- for work in gallery.works %}
            <li class="slide" id="{{ work.id }}">
                <div
                    class="slide-group"
                    role="group"
                    aria-roledescription="slide"
                    aria-label="Work {{ work.position }} of {{ gallery.works | length }}"
                >
                    <picture>
                        <source
                            type="image/webp"
                            srcset="{{ work.srcsetWebp }}"
                            sizes="(min-width: 40rem) 610px, calc(100vw - 30px)"
                        />
                        <img
                            src="{{ work.src }}"
                            srcset="{{ work.srcsetJpeg }}"
                            sizes="(min-width: 40rem) 610px, calc(100vw - 30px)"
                            width="{{ work.width }}"
                            height="{{ work.height }}"
                            alt="{{ work.alt }}"
                            loading="{{ 'eager' if loop.first else 'lazy' }}"
                            fetchpriority="{{ 'high' if loop.first else 'auto' }}"
                            decoding="async"
                        />
                    </picture>
                </div>
            </li>
            {%- endfor %}
        </ul>
    </div>

    {# The two sizes values above and below are the real layout boxes, not guesses, and YEO-138
    rewrote both when it made the boxes fluid. #slideshow is min(610px, 100vw - 30px) - the two
    cross at 640px, which is the 40rem above. A pager cell is 20% of .gallery-page's inner width
    less its 10px gutter: 870px inner once the 900px cap bites (164px), and (100vw - 30px) below
    that, which reduces to calc(20vw - 16px). Both conditions are min-width, like every breakpoint
    in the stylesheet. The pager is plain anchors to each slide's fragment. With
    scroll-behavior:smooth on the scroller the browser brings the slide into view natively, so the
    pager works fully with JavaScript disabled - which the Cycle2 pager did not. js/gallery.js only
    adds the active highlight and arrow keys on top. The thumbnails are alt="" deliberately. They
    are the same 33 works already described above, and repeating each description would make a
    screen reader announce every work twice; the link's own aria-label names it instead. They are
    still real img elements, so no CSS background-image pointing at /img/ survives anywhere in the
    output. #}
    <nav id="pager" aria-label="Choose a work">
        {%- for work in gallery.works %}
        <a href="#{{ work.id }}" aria-label="Work {{ work.position }}">
            <picture>
                <source
                    type="image/webp"
                    srcset="{{ work.srcsetWebp }}"
                    sizes="(min-width: 56.25rem) 164px, calc(20vw - 16px)"
                />
                <img
                    src="{{ work.src }}"
                    srcset="{{ work.srcsetJpeg }}"
                    sizes="(min-width: 56.25rem) 164px, calc(20vw - 16px)"
                    width="{{ work.width }}"
                    height="{{ work.height }}"
                    alt=""
                    loading="lazy"
                    decoding="async"
                />
            </picture>
        </a>
        {%- endfor %}
    </nav>
</div>
